, Elector of Bavaria\",\"Maximilian II Emanuel of Bavaria\",\"Mehmed IV\",\"Melvyn Bragg\",\"Michael I Apafi\",\"Miecław's Rebellion\",\"Mihaly Apafi\",\"Mikołaj Hieronim Sieniawski\",\"Military history of Poland during World War II\",\"Military of the Ottoman Empire\",\"Military of the Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth\",\"Mining (military)\",\"Moldavia\",\"Moldavian Magnate Wars\",\"Moldavian campaign (1497–1499)\",\"Moldavian campaign (1684–1691)\",\"Murad Giray\",\"Muscovite–Lithuanian Wars\",\"Nalyvaiko Uprising\",\"Napoleonic Wars\",\"Nativity of Mary\",\"Naval actions at the siege of Ochakov (1788)\"],\"blacklist\":[],\"lastLinkClicked\":\"Battle of Vienna\"},\"feedback\":1.026145210363524},{\"t\":1,\"state\":{\"currentTitle\":\"Battle of Vienna\",\"summary\":\"The Siege and Battle of Vienna took place at Kahlenberg Mountain near Vienna on 12 September 1683 after the city had been besieged by the Ottoman Empire for two months. The battle was fought by the Holy Roman Empire, led by the Habsburg monarchy, and the Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth, both under the command of Polish King John III Sobieski, against the Ottomans and their vassal and tributary states. The battle marked the first time the Commonwealth and the Holy Roman Empire had cooperated militarily against the Ottomans. The defeat was a major turning point for the Ottoman Empire and their expansion into Europe, after which they would gain no further ground. In the ensuing war that lasted until 1699, the Ottomans would cede most of Ottoman Hungary to Leopold I, Holy Roman Emperor.\\nThe battle was won by the combined forces of the Holy Roman Empire and the Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth, the latter represented only by the forces of the Crown of the Kingdom of Poland, the arrival of the Lithuanian army was delayed, and only reached Vienna after it had been relieved. The Viennese garrison was led by the General of the Artillery of the Holy Roman Imperial Army Count Starhemberg, an Austrian subject of Holy Roman Emperor Leopold I. The overall command was held by the senior leader, the King of Poland, John III Sobieski, who led the relief forces.\\nThe forces of the Ottoman Empire and its vassal states were commanded by Grand Vizier Merzifonlu Kara Mustafa Pasha. The Ottoman army numbered approximately 90,000 to 300,000 men, and according to the documents on the order of battle found in Kara Mustafa's tent, initial strength at the start of the campaign was 170,000 men. They began the siege on 14 July 1683. Ottoman forces consisted, among other units, of 60 ortas of Janissaries, numbering 1,000 men on paper, with an observation army of some 70,000 men guarding the countryside. The decisive battle took place on 12 September, after the arrival of the united relief army.\\nSome historians maintain that the battle marked a turning point in the Ottoman–Habsburg wars, a 300-year struggle between the Holy Roman and Ottoman Empires. During the 16 years following the battle, the Austrian Habsburgs would gradually conquer southern Hungary and Transylvania, largely clearing them of Ottoman forces. The battle is noted for including the largest known cavalry charge in history.\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle%20of%20Vienna\",\"goal\":\"French Revolution\",\"history\":[\"Battle of Vienna\"],\"depth\":1,\"links\":[\"2003 invasion of Iraq\",\"Action of 6 July 1697\",\"Adana campaign\",\"Aeon (digital magazine)\",\"Al-Hasa Expedition 1871\",\"Al-Qaeda\",\"Algiers expedition (1541)\",\"Anti-Turkish sentiment\",\"Archduchy of Austria\",\"Army of observation\",\"Army of the Holy Roman Empire\",\"Artur Grottger\",\"August Querfurt\",\"Austria\",\"Austro-Polish War\",\"Azov campaigns\",\"Azov campaigns (1695–96)\",\"Baghdad\",\"Balkan Peninsula\",\"Bar Confederation\",\"Bastion\",\"Battle at Jurjeve Stijene\",\"Battle of Abukir (1799)\",\"Battle of Akhaltsikhe\",\"Battle of Akhaltsikhe (1853)\",\"Battle of Al-Safra\",\"Battle of Al Wajbah\",\"Battle of Aladzha\",\"Battle of A